WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.000
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here, and today
I want to talk about learning words.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.440
Uh, why?

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:11.850
Well, one reason is that, uh, I
started up polish about 10 days ago,

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:18.060
and I was notified by LingQ that
I now know 36,000 words in Polish.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:23.610
Uh, I didn't do 30, didn't
learn 36,000 words in 10 days.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.730
Uh, back in 2015, I, I can't remember
if it was three months or six months,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.910
but certainly I was very active during a
three month period, uh, with Polish and

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.269
doing a lot of Polish history, starting
of course with our mini stories and,

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:44.879
and simpler content, and then building
up to, to the history, uh, of Poland,

00:00:44.879 --> 00:00:46.200
which was of great interest to me.

00:00:46.769 --> 00:00:50.090
But this reminded me, wow, 36,000.

00:00:51.045 --> 00:00:54.495
I don't necessarily speak that
well, but I understand a lot.

00:00:54.615 --> 00:01:00.915
I have a base in the language and it
reminded me that, um, I've always felt

00:01:01.005 --> 00:01:07.695
that the simplest measure of our level in
the language doesn't necessarily mean our

00:01:07.695 --> 00:01:11.745
ability to speak, but our level in that
language, our potential in that language

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:14.805
is the number of words that we know.

00:01:15.195 --> 00:01:18.890
And so that's what I want to talk about
today, how do we learn these words?

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.080
What does it mean to know words?

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:22.910
Uh, and so forth.

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:29.780
I remember when I wrote my book back in
2003, uh, the Linguist on Language: A

00:01:30.169 --> 00:01:35.030
Language Learning Odyssey, I remember
saying two things there that at that

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.160
time I felt very strongly about.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.449
This was before I had ever heard of
Stephen Krashen and the input hypothesis

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:47.539
before I had heard of the three keys:
your attitude, the time you spend with

00:01:47.539 --> 00:01:50.190
the language and the ability to notice.

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:54.929
I didn't know any of those things,
but I felt that two things were at

00:01:54.929 --> 00:01:59.009
least part of my success in learning
Chinese, which I learned in less than

00:01:59.009 --> 00:02:03.419
a year, up to a level of, let's say
4,000 characters reading newspapers,

00:02:03.419 --> 00:02:07.979
translating from Chinese to English,
from English to Chinese, uh, you know,

00:02:07.979 --> 00:02:09.840
newspaper editorials and things like that.

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:16.965
That one was intensity, and the other
was the number of words I had to learn.

00:02:17.885 --> 00:02:21.795
The sort of Gordian Knot measurement
of where you stand in the languages,

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:27.045
the number of words, you know, and
that's why when we built LingQ, we

00:02:27.045 --> 00:02:31.035
built into it this measurement of words.

00:02:31.215 --> 00:02:35.235
Words that you save, which is the number
of LingQs created, and words that you

00:02:35.235 --> 00:02:40.155
know, which means words that the system
has decided that you already know.

00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:46.079
Now, sometimes when I say this I
get criticism because, you know,

00:02:46.079 --> 00:02:47.820
what does it mean to know a word?

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.150
Uh, you don't know the
full scope of the word.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.950
Uh, you can't use the word.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.519
All of this is true, but those
things are also difficult to measure.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.859
What we can measure at LingQ is whether
you looked up the word in a given text.

00:03:01.859 --> 00:03:05.070
If you didn't look it up, the
assumption is, you know it.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:10.710
And so the process for me of learning
words is not sort of pouring over lists,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:16.740
studying, uh, vocabulary, feeling that
sort of urgency to learn this vocabulary.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.950
Those activities are
largely self-defeating.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:24.360
The more you review your, um, at least
in my own experience, flashcards,

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.650
the more you review lists, the
less is sticking in your brain.

00:03:29.445 --> 00:03:34.935
The more you just expose yourself to
content reading and listening and just

00:03:34.935 --> 00:03:39.405
throwing these words into your brain,
eventually those words will start

00:03:39.405 --> 00:03:44.805
to initially become known and then
eventually you'll be able to use them.

00:03:44.805 --> 00:03:45.525
So how does it work?

00:03:46.575 --> 00:03:48.495
So at LingQ, all the words are blue.

00:03:49.185 --> 00:03:53.955
At first, you look up the words
you don't know, and I see it as

00:03:53.955 --> 00:03:56.795
kind of taking those blue words
and throwing them into my brain.

00:03:57.245 --> 00:03:59.880
And the minute they're into
my brain, they're now yellow.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:01.200
In other words, I've clicked on them.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:02.810
They have become LingQs.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:09.690
Uh, in time these yellow LingQs in
my brain and also in my database

00:04:09.695 --> 00:04:15.120
at LingQ will convert themselves
into known words, because in time I

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.380
will read, say other contexts where
that yellow word appears again.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:25.260
Or I will save words that are very
similar, and because these similar

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.680
words are kind of side by side, all
of a sudden I start to know them.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.950
In fact, increasingly, words
that I have never seen before

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.190
that are similar to words that I
know, I throw them in my brain.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.100
Or they automatically get into my brain.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.480
I don't even have to save them.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:45.120
And so that my vocabulary grows,
it grows because I do a lot of

00:04:45.125 --> 00:04:51.750
listening and reading and so it's
kind of a mutual relationship.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:56.490
The, uh, more you read and the more
you listen, the more words you know.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:02.700
Therefore, this measurement of how many
words you know, or have looked up is a

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.800
measurement of your level of activity.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.430
It typically means that you have
read a large number of words.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.780
You have listened to a lot of,
uh, the language, because I

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.030
always listen to what I'm reading.

00:05:15.659 --> 00:05:19.700
And again, you can see this
from my statistics at LingQ.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:25.890
Unfortunately, um, initially when
I, I start into a language, I use

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.480
content in our LingQ library, but
increasingly I import content from

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.730
elsewhere and therefore I have the
text in LingQ and that enables link

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.960
to measure my activity in LingQ.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:43.020
But typically the audiobook, the
audio for that is somewhere else.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:48.360
So I'm doing that in iTunes or I'm
doing it in, uh, maybe the audiobook

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:53.400
app and therefore, I'm not measuring it
automatically at LingQ, but typically

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:58.169
knowing a lot of words means that you
have thrown a lot of words into this

00:05:58.169 --> 00:06:00.390
database, into this memory reserve.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:05.070
Even if you can't retrieve them,
slowly those words are going to

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:09.719
become, even initially you're gonna
forget that you ever saw the word,

00:06:09.719 --> 00:06:11.280
but it's already in your database.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.299
It's already in your reserve,
and over time it become a white

00:06:15.299 --> 00:06:18.359
word, which means you kind of
understand it in most contexts.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:23.250
And in time some of those words will
become, you know, words that you can use.

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:29.370
So to me, this is sort of the fundamental
task is, is gathering all these

00:06:29.375 --> 00:06:33.719
words, coming across them in different
contexts, throwing them into your

00:06:33.719 --> 00:06:39.030
brain, letting them gradually evolve
naturally into words that you understand.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.840
And eventually, and I should say, you
understand and you gradually get a,

00:06:43.059 --> 00:06:48.150
a better sense of the, the scope of
meaning of those words, you get a better

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:51.989
sense of which words are used with the
words that you've put into your brain.

00:06:51.989 --> 00:06:54.390
So just heave them in there
and don't worry about them.

00:06:54.599 --> 00:06:56.039
Don't worry about forgetting them.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:57.929
Just throw them into your brain.

00:06:58.469 --> 00:07:00.150
Uh, and that to me is the process.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:04.440
Now, some people may say, uh,
what's the point of knowing so

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:06.420
many words if you can't speak?

00:07:06.795 --> 00:07:10.515
Uh, and obviously whatever
we do in language learning

00:07:10.545 --> 00:07:11.985
is a function of our goals.

00:07:12.015 --> 00:07:16.635
So if I have a limited sort of demand
of, you know, what I need to be

00:07:16.635 --> 00:07:20.895
able to do in a certain language,
uh, I wanna focus in on being

00:07:20.895 --> 00:07:24.975
able to have simple conversations,
then I can limit my vocabulary

00:07:25.045 --> 00:07:28.425
to, um, you know, a limited range.

00:07:28.965 --> 00:07:30.725
It'll work in a lot of situations.

00:07:31.049 --> 00:07:35.789
Unfortunately, the native speaker may
use words that are outside the range

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:39.750
of words that you have tried to focus
on, and where you have developed an

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:44.429
ability to converse in that limited
range, but it certainly serves a purpose.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.880
But that is not typically
what I aspire to do.

00:07:48.179 --> 00:07:53.909
I aspire to acquire a lot of words
because typically what I'm going to

00:07:53.909 --> 00:07:57.479
be able to do with the language that
I'm learning is listen and read.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.520
Read about history as I'm doing now.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.080
I'm, it's, it's just fascinating.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.535
I'm reading and listening to a book
about the history of Poland, starting

00:08:05.535 --> 00:08:10.275
basically at the time of the partition
of Poland through to modern times.

00:08:10.335 --> 00:08:11.625
It's very interesting.

00:08:11.685 --> 00:08:15.495
I did the same with Danish, so
that's a big part of my enjoyment

00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:17.925
of the language when I go to Poland.

00:08:17.985 --> 00:08:21.225
I wanna speak and of course, the
more I speak the better I will speak.

00:08:21.765 --> 00:08:25.545
Uh, I will leave you a video
of me, uh, speaking in Polish

00:08:25.575 --> 00:08:27.625
when I was active in 2015.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:29.460
Can I do as well now?

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.180
I don't know.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:34.680
I just had a person contact me through,
through, uh, Twitter and Skype, and we had

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.840
a brief conversation in halting Polish.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:39.780
I think, you know, I've been
at Polish now for 10 days.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:44.250
Uh, it'll be two months of working
with Polish before I get to Poland.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.870
I think I'll be able to do quite well
and I will certainly, uh, make a video

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:54.420
when I'm in Poland, but I'm realistic,
you know, I will have a week in Poland.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:57.030
My polish will improve while I'm there.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:01.785
And then when I come home and I no
longer speak Polish regularly, my

00:09:01.785 --> 00:09:06.735
Polish ability to speak will decline,
but I will still have the vocabulary

00:09:06.855 --> 00:09:11.715
that I acquired and I can always go
back as I'm doing now, go back to what

00:09:11.715 --> 00:09:18.525
I studied, uh, in 2015, or explore new
material where a lot of the words that

00:09:18.525 --> 00:09:22.535
I saved, that I threw into my brain
that became yellow and possibly white.

00:09:22.875 --> 00:09:26.175
A lot of these words will
naturally reappear in the new

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:27.705
content that I'm learning from.

00:09:27.975 --> 00:09:31.365
And so all of the words that I've
saved and put into my reserve,

00:09:31.455 --> 00:09:33.075
I have those, those are mine.

00:09:33.615 --> 00:09:36.345
My ability to converse will fluctuate.

00:09:36.915 --> 00:09:43.255
And so therefore, to me, the
my measurement, the simplest

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:47.695
measurement of where I am in a
language is how many words I know.

00:09:48.605 --> 00:09:54.195
Uh, how many words I put into my reserve,
my ability to speak, particularly in, in

00:09:54.195 --> 00:09:58.125
those languages where I haven't spoken
a lot it's gonna fluctuate 'cause I've

00:09:58.125 --> 00:10:01.935
said many times to be very good on
the language you have to speak a lot.

00:10:02.025 --> 00:10:06.630
And those languages that I speak
the best where I lose, you know,

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:08.790
less of my ability to converse.

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:10.830
Those are the languages
that I've spoken a lot.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.610
And so Poland, it's not because
of one week in Poland gonna

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:17.760
fall into the category of
languages that I've spoken a lot.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:22.860
But I think by focusing in on
words, listening and reading, I'm

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:24.330
learning a lot about the country.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:27.270
It's gonna make my visit
there much more rewarding.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:29.880
I'm going to be able to
converse with people in Polish.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:34.110
Uh, my ability to speak will improve
while I'm there, and then it'll start to

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:39.089
decline again unless I decide to spend
a lot of time, uh, working on Polish

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:42.959
and neglecting Persian and Arabic in
the other languages that I'm working on.

00:10:43.079 --> 00:10:44.370
So there you have it.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.030
Learning words shouldn't be stressful.

00:10:48.089 --> 00:10:49.709
It shouldn't be a deliberate activity.

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:54.480
It should be a part of your gradual
immersion into the language, the

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.010
culture, the history, and all those
things that make every language

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.410
so, thank you for listening.

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:00.100
Bye for now.

